SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     dtrace(1), boottrace(4), ktr(4)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     ttsslloogg first appeared in FreeBSD 12.0.  Support for tracing boot loaders
     and userland process was added in FreeBSD 13.2.

   TTSSLLOOGG vvss.. BBoooottttrraaccee
     ttsslloogg is oriented towards system developers while boottrace(4) is meant
     to be easy to use by system administrators.  Both faciliities provide an
     overview of timing and resource usage of the boot process.

   TTSSLLOOGG vvss.. DDTTrraaccee
     dtrace(1) is not always the right tool for profiling early kernel ini‐
     tialization.  The reason is it requires some kernel subroutines which are
     not yet available early in the boot process, e.g.: traps, memory alloca‐
     tion, or thread scheduling.  ttsslloogg depends on fewer kernel subroutines
     than dtrace(1) and because of that can trace early kernel initialization.

   TTSSLLOOGG vvss.. KKTTRR
     ktr(4) has a couple of limitations which prevent it from being able to
     run at the start of the boot process.  In contrast, ttsslloogg is designed for
     logging timestamped events for boot profiling.

